    <h3 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.4;">IBA‑Approved Consignment Bilty:</h3>
    <p style="color: #94a3b8; font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">The Indian Bureau of Shipping (IBA) or an equivalent maritime authority must approve the consignment bilty. This bilty acts as a contract of carriage, outlining the terms and conditions of the transport, the route, and the responsibilities of both parties.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>One of the most critical aspects of the paperwork is the <strong>100% declared‑value marine transit insurance</strong>. This type of insurance ensures that the vehicle’s full market value is covered against loss or damage during the journey, whether it is transported by road, rail, or sea.</p>
<p>By declaring the vehicle’s value in full, the consignor eliminates the risk of under‑insurance and secures a higher payout in the event of an unfortunate incident. Transport operators often require proof of such coverage before signing the consignment bilty, as it protects both the owner and the carrier from potential liabilities.</p>

<p>In summary, a well‑prepared set of documents—including a self‑attested RC photocopy, a current PUC, a valid insurance certificate, and an IBA‑approved consignment bilty—combined with 100% declared‑value marine transit insurance, provides a robust framework for a safe and compliant car transport from Patna to Mumbai.</p></div>
